<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Mountain Wreath</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1847">1847</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mountain Wreath is a verse drama by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petar-ii-petrovic-njegos">Petar II Petrović-Njegoš</a>, prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a>, published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1847. Set around 1700, it depicts the Montenegrin struggle against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and the killing of local converts to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and it is regarded as a masterpiece of Serbian and Montenegrin literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-mountain-wreath.jpg" alt="The Mountain Wreath" width="320" height="524" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Petar II Petrović-Njegoš · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gorski_Vijenac.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petar-ii-petrovic-njegos">Njegoš</a> wrote the work in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetinje">Cetinje</a> during 1846 and had it printed the next year at an Armenian monastery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. It is a modern epic in verse written as a play, so combining three of the major modes of literary expression. It is built around an allegedly historical event of Metropolitan Danilo's rule, the mass execution at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> of Montenegrins who had converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, known as the Inquisition of the Turkicized (Istraga Poturica). A recent History of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> holds that such an event occurred in 1707 but was highly localised, confined to the Ćeklići clan, and some Balkan historians doubt the massacre happened at all.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The poem follows <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petar-ii-petrovic-njegos">Njegoš</a>'s ancestor Metropolitan Danilo I Petrović-Njegoš as he tries to regulate relations among the warring tribes. It opens with Danilo's vision of the spread of Turkish power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>; torn by inner conflict, he sees that the struggle is inevitable but dreads its outcome. In scenes of dialogue and monologue the drama widens into pictures of Montenegrin feasts, gatherings, customs and beliefs, and two assemblies of chieftains, one on the eve of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pentecost">Pentecost</a> and one on the Nativity of the Virgin Mary, resolve to drive out the Turks.</p><p>Its 2,819 verses set three opposing civilisations side by side: patriarchal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. Written in the decasyllabic metre and language of Serbian epic folk poetry, it gave many lines that became proverbs, such as &quot;When things go well 'tis easy to be good, / In suffering one learns who is the hero!&quot;</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The historian Srđa Pavlović notes that each generation of South Slav historians and politicians has drawn on the poem to support opposing views. Michael Sells, a professor of Islamic history and literature, points out that it was required reading in all the schools of pre-war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> and was memorised and quoted by radical Serb nationalists of the 1990s, and the journalist Tim Judah called it &quot;a paean to ethnic cleansing&quot;. Pavlović proposes reading it instead as a tale of a long-gone tribal society, set in the context of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petar-ii-petrovic-njegos">Njegoš</a>'s own time and his efforts to advance <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pan-slavism">pan-Slavism</a> and the Illyrian Movement.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “The Mountain Wreath” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Petar I defeated Kara Mahmud Pasha Bushati in 1796, and Petar II, author of the epic <b>The Mountain Wreath</b>, created a senate and taxes.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-montenegro">Prince-Bishopric of Montenegro</a></li></ul></section>
